#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151865 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152062 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1576ea0-ab01-5d32-81b1-782024bdcc98" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Additive Inverses Modulo N"}] :question/id #uuid "b12c927f-5cfd-5c8d-876e-6995d17d2a99" :question/math-academy-id "e-14655" :question/problem "Is the following statement true or false?\n\n> The integer $21$ is the additive inverse of $14$ modulo $35$." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $14$ modulo $35$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 35\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\nx + 14 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:35)\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is congruent to zero modulo $35{:}$\n\n$$\n21 + 14 \\mid ≡35\\begin{vmatrix}(mod35) \\\\ ≡0\\end{vmatrix}(mod35)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the statement is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151866 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5513a4ce-730e-56fd-ac8c-8ee1b002abfc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Number"}] :question/id #uuid "b7f4fa4a-b123-514d-ae6e-6482805e0168" :question/math-academy-id "e-14659" :question/problem "Find the additive inverse of $17$ modulo $26$." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $17$ modulo $26$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 26\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\nx + 17 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)\n$$\n\nNotice that $-17 + 17 \\equiv 0$, so $x\\equiv -17\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$. To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\nx \\mid ≡ - 17\\begin{vmatrix}(mod26) \\\\ ≡0 - 17\\end{vmatrix}(mod26); ≡26 - 17\\begin{vmatrix}(mod26) \\\\ ≡9\\end{vmatrix}(mod26)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $17$ modulo $26$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$.\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n$9 + 17 \\equiv 26 \\equiv 0\\:\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$\n\n**Note:** The additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n$, i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151867 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f995e0b-cb5a-511a-98da-3d77b3369d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Larger Number"}] :question/id #uuid "25638e23-0a57-53d6-9451-ff1cf133d80e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14660" :question/problem "Find the additive inverse of $23$ modulo $15$." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $23$ modulo $15$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 15\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\nx + 23 \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15)\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $23\\equiv 8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15){:}$\n$23 \\mid ≡1 \\cdot 15 + 8\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡1 \\cdot 0 + 8\\end{vmatrix}(mod15); ≡8 \\mid (mod15)$\n\nAlso, note that $-8 + 8 \\equiv 0$, so $x\\equiv -8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$. To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\nx \\mid ≡ - 8\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡0 - 8\\end{vmatrix}(mod15); ≡15 - 8\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡7\\end{vmatrix}(mod15)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $23$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}$.\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n$23 + 7≡30≡2 \\cdot 15≡0✓(mod15)$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n$, i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151868 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c91373a9-4a84-52ea-a77a-16beabf09acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Negative Number"}] :question/id #uuid "e68d2ba3-b6d2-5906-a8d0-f22b70eeb171" :question/math-academy-id "e-14661" :question/problem "Find the additive inverse of $-43$ modulo $15$." :question/worked-solution "The additive inverse of $-43$ modulo $15$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 15\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\nx + (-43) \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15)\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $-43\\equiv 2\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15){:}$\n$-43 \\mid ≡(-3) \\cdot 15 + 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡(-3) \\cdot 0 + 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod15); ≡2 \\mid (mod15)$\n\nAlso, note that $-2 + 2 \\equiv 0$, so $x\\equiv -2\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:15)$. To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\nx \\mid ≡ - 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡0 - 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod15); ≡15 - 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod15) \\\\ ≡13\\end{vmatrix}(mod15)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-43$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}13}$.\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n$-43 + 13≡ - 30≡(-2) \\cdot 15≡0✓(mod15)$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n$, i.e. $-a \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: n$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152064 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f995e0b-cb5a-511a-98da-3d77b3369d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Larger Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "8e15f803-1fdf-51c2-bc72-db4559a3078b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149518}] :question/id #uuid "29a5c0fc-899a-52da-8f72-1b9fc79f6f90" :question/math-academy-id "q-213236" :question/problem "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $16$, the additive inverse of $45$ modulo $16$ is {{field-1}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c91373a9-4a84-52ea-a77a-16beabf09acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "00abe795-6125-5b71-8af0-72616318b270" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-fie